%I A401051 #59 Oct 20 2026 23:35:21
%S A401051 3,3,17,2,19,3,31,11,113,67,127,79,131,53,157,139,179,37,223,163,227,
%T A401051 31,229,197,241,2,251,163,257,7,281,59,293,43,331,41,349,151,421,223,
%U A401051 467,409,79,479,41,563,331,571,281,593,359,641,71,647,443,673,241,823,197,859,503,907,241,911,17,1013,941,1039,149,1091,719
%N A401051 Irregular triangle read by rows: T(n,1) = A400843(n), T(n,k>1) = the (k-1)-th largest prime q <= T(n,1) such that 1+2*((T(n,1)#/T(n,1)) / (q#/q)) is a multiple of T(n,1), with n >= 1, 1 <= k <= 1+A401053(A401059(n)). Here p# = A034386(p).
%C A401051 After the initial row (3, 3), this table lists such pairs of primes p and q, p > q, for which the products p^2 * q (given in A401228) satisfy the condition that A276085(p^2 * q) is a multiple of p.
%H A401051 Antti Karttunen, <a href="/A401051/b401051.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..8413; the first 4102 rows of the triangle</a>
%H A401051 <a href="/index/Pri#primorial_numbers">Index entries for sequences related to primorial numbers</a>.
%e A401051 The first 35 rows are:
%e A401051      3, 3;
